    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-m/q3",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-m/q3-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The table below summarizes census information about the number of children in the households of an English town. Number of children 0 1 2 3 4 or more Percentage of households 23 32 35 7 3 A random sample of 20 households is selected from this town. a) Determine the probability that the sample will contain ... ( ) 2 i. ... 3 households with no children. ( ) ii. ... more than half the households, with at least 2 children. 2\" style=\"--madas-width:588.0px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-m/q3-question-2.svg\" alt=\"iii. ... more than 15 but at most 19 households, with at most 2 children. ( ) 2 A new random sample of n households is selected from this town. The probability that this new sample contains a household with 4 or more children is more than 10% . ( ) 5 b) Determine the smallest value of n .\" style=\"--madas-width:604.1px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-m/q3-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"(a) Model each count by a binomial distribution with 20 trials and the relevant population probability. (i) For X ∼B(20, 0.23), 20 ! (0.23)3(0.77)17 ≈0.1631 . P(X = 3) = [M1] [A1] 3 (ii) The probability of at least two children is 0.35 + 0.07 + 0.03 = 0.45. [M1] For Y ∼B(20, 0.45), P(Y ≥11) = 1 −P(Y ≤10) ≈0.2493 . [A1] (iii) The probability of at most two children is 0.90. [M1] For W ∼B(20, 0.90), P(16 ≤W ≤19) = P(W ≤19) −P(W ≤15) ≈0.8352 . [A1] (b) The complement has probability 0.97n. [B1] We require 0.97n &lt; 0.90, [A1] Equivalently, n &gt; log(0.90)/ log(0.97) ≈3.459, [A1] with the inequality reversed upon dividing by the negative logarithm: [A1] Check the neighbouring integers directly: 0.973 = 0.912673 &gt; 0.90, whereas\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-m/q3-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"0.974 = 0.88529 . . . &lt; 0.90. The smallest integer is 4 . [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:441.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 3 is 11 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "The table below summarizes census information about the number of children in the households of an English town. Number of children 0 1 2 3 4 or more Percentage of households 23 32 35 7 3 A random sample of 20 households is selected from this town. a) Determine the probability that the sample will contain ... ( ) 2 i. ... 3 households with no children. ( ) ii. ... more than half the households, with at least 2 children. 2 iii. ... more than 15 but at most 19 households, with at most 2 children. ( ) 2 A new random sample of n households is selected from this town. The probability that this new sample contains a household with 4 or more children is more than 10% . ( ) 5 b) Determine the smallest value of n ."
  },
