    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-m/q6",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-m/q6-question-1.svg\" alt=\"William has been established over a long period of time that when he shoots an arrow at a target the probability of hitting it, is 0.35. William buys a professional bow as he believes it will increase the probability of hitting the target. Let X define the number of successful hits of the target, and assume that successful or non successful hits are independent of one another. a) Determine a critical region based on 20 shots with the new bow. ( ) 6 The significance level must be as close as possible to 1% . William decides to carry out a significance test by shooting 150 arrows at the target using the new bow. He finds that 59 arrows hit the target.\" style=\"--madas-width:576.0px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-m/q6-question-2.svg\" alt=\"b) Use a distributional approximation, to calculate an approximate p -value and ( ) 6 hence state the conclusion in context.\" style=\"--madas-width:590.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-m/q6-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Let p be the probability of hitting the target with the new bow: Test H0 : p = 0.35 against H1 : p &gt; 0.35. (a) Under H0, X ∼B(20, 0.35). [B1] [A1] The probabilities P(X ≥12) ≈0.01958 and P(X ≥13) ≈0.00602 bracket 0.01. [M1] [A1] The latter is closer to 0.01, giving the region X ≥13 . [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:680.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-m/q6-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(b) For 150 shots use Y ∼N(52.5, 34.125) [M1] [A1] as an approximation under H0. [A1] For 150 trials the mean is 150(0.35) = 52.5; the variance is 150(0.35)(0.65) = 34.125. At the lower boundary √ z = 6/ 34.125 . = 1.027 . . . With the continuity correction, 58.5 −52.5  p-value = P(X ≥59) ≈P(Y ≥58.5) = 1 −Φ √ ≈0.1522 . [M1] [A1] 34.125 There is insufficient evidence at the 1% level that the new bow increases the probability of hitting the target: [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:678.7px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 6 is 12 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "William has been established over a long period of time that when he shoots an arrow at a target the probability of hitting it, is 0.35. William buys a professional bow as he believes it will increase the probability of hitting the target. Let X define the number of successful hits of the target, and assume that successful or non successful hits are independent of one another. a) Determine a critical region based on 20 shots with the new bow. ( ) 6 The significance level must be as close as possible to 1% . William decides to carry out a significance test by shooting 150 arrows at the target using the new bow. He finds that 59 arrows hit the target. b) Use a distributional approximation, to calculate an approximate p -value and ( ) 6 hence state the conclusion in context."
  },
  {
    "id": "madas/alevel-applied-m/q7",
    "question": "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-m/q7-question-1.svg\" alt=\"The scheduled flight DM104 , of a certain airline, can be delayed due to the following three reasons. • aircraft technical problems, denoted by the event T • weather conditions, denoted by the event W • air traffic congestion, denoted by the event C . These events are assumed to be independent of one another and the flight will be delayed if one or more of these events occur. It is given that ( ) P T = 0.1 , ( P W = ) 0.05 and ( ) P C = 0.2 . a) Find the probability that the next DM104 flight …\" style=\"--madas-width:565.3px\"></figure><figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-m/q7-question-2.svg\" alt=\"( ) 3 i. … will be delayed due to exactly one reason. ( ) 3 ii.  … will be delayed. b) Given that the next DM104 flight was delayed, find the probability that it was delayed due to one reason only. ( ) 3 c) Given that the next DM104 flight had no technical problems, find the probability that it was delayed. ( ) 3\" style=\"--madas-width:582.7px\"></figure>",
    "steps": [
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-m/q7-solution-1.svg\" alt=\"Drawing a tree diagram:\" style=\"--madas-width:514.7px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-m/q7-solution-2.svg\" alt=\"(a) By independence, (i) the probability of exactly one reason is 0.1(0.95)(0.8) + 0.9(0.05)(0.8) + 0.9(0.95)(0.2) = 0.283 , [M1] [M1] [A1] (ii) the delay probability is 1 −(0.9)(0.95)(0.8) = 0.316 . [M1] [M1] [A1] (b) Exactly one reason implies a delay: [B1] The three single-cause contributions are 0.076, 0.036 and 0.171. The no-delay route has probability 0.684. Restrict the denominator to the complementary event of a delay: The conditional probability is 0.283/0.316 ≈0.896 . [M1] [A1] (c) Given T ′, the independent probabilities of W, C remain unchanged: [B1] The full conditional fraction from the tree is P(T ′ ∩(W ∪C))/ P(T ′).\" style=\"--madas-width:680.0px\"></figure>",
      "<figure class=\"madas-excerpt\"><img src=\"resources/madas/alevel-applied-m/q7-solution-3.svg\" alt=\"The numerator is 0.9[0.05(0.2) + 0.05(0.8) + 0.95(0.2)] = 0.216; divide by 0.9. The required probability is 1 −0.95(0.8) = 0.24 . [M1] [A1]\" style=\"--madas-width:677.3px\"></figure>",
      "<p class=\"madas-total\">Total mark for question 7 is 12 marks.</p>"
    ],
    "promptText": "The scheduled flight DM104 , of a certain airline, can be delayed due to the following three reasons. • aircraft technical problems, denoted by the event T • weather conditions, denoted by the event W • air traffic congestion, denoted by the event C . These events are assumed to be independent of one another and the flight will be delayed if one or more of these events occur. It is given that ( ) P T = 0.1 , ( P W = ) 0.05 and ( ) P C = 0.2 . a) Find the probability that the next DM104 flight … ( ) 3 i. … will be delayed due to exactly one reason. ( ) 3 ii.  … will be delayed. b) Given that the next DM104 flight was delayed, find the probability that it was delayed due to one reason only. ( ) 3 c) Given that the next DM104 flight had no technical problems, find the probability that it was delayed. ( ) 3"
  },
